@@ -0,0 +1,36 @@
+# The Daily Grind
+
+The coffee shop page now includes server-side email ownership verification at `/account`, `/rewards`, and `/`. Supporting browsers populate the hidden Email Verification API field. The server verifies the SD-JWT and browser key binding; empty or invalid tokens automatically continue with a six-digit email code.
+
+## Run
+
+Use Node.js 22 or newer.
+
+```sh
+pnpm install
+npm run build
+npm test
+npm start
+```
+
+The default address is `http://localhost:11222`. Set `PORT` to change the port. For local browser testing, use localhost: the session cookie always uses `HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax` and a `__Host-` prefix. HTTP on arbitrary hosts cannot set that cookie; deployed sites must use HTTPS.
+
+## Configuration
+
+| Variable | Purpose |
+| --- | --- |
+| `RP_ORIGIN` | Exact public origin, such as `https://coffee.example.com`; mandatory in production. Used as the expected KB-JWT audience and for Origin checks. Never derived from untrusted request headers. |
+| `ORIGIN_TRIAL_TOKEN` | Your browser-issued Origin Trial token for the public origin. Sent through the `Origin-Trial` response header. Without a valid token, the email-code flow still works. |
+| `SMTP_URL` | Nodemailer SMTP connection URL. Mandatory in production; use your email provider’s authenticated SMTP service. |
+| `MAIL_FROM` | Verified sender address for your SMTP provider. |
+| `NODE_ENV` | Set to `production` for a deployment. |
+
+During local development without SMTP, outgoing messages are saved as private `.mail/*.eml` files. Read the newest message to obtain the code. Codes are never exposed in the browser response. This is a development mailbox, not delivery to an actual inbox. Production startup requires SMTP configuration; delivery failures show a retry form.
+
+## Verification and state
+
+`@sd-jwt/core` parses disclosures and validates `sd_hash`, nonce, audience, and key-binding freshness. `jose` verifies the issuer and holder signatures with an asymmetric algorithm allowlist. DNS delegation is checked before issuer discovery; issuer metadata must exactly match `iss`. A missing `kid` tries every eligible JWKS key. Email comparisons are case-insensitive while retaining the submitted address.
+
+The server generates a 256-bit nonce for every email form render. Session cookies contain only a random identifier. Nonces live in process memory, expire after five minutes, and are synchronously read and deleted at POST receipt, before reading the request body. Responses use `Cache-Control: no-store`. Email codes expire in ten minutes, are stored as keyed hashes, allow five attempts, and are single-use. Sending is throttled per session and client IP.
+
+This implementation runs as one Node.js process. For multiple workers or replicas, replace the in-memory session store with a shared store and atomic nonce consumption (for example Redis `GETDEL`), and move throttling into that store. Restarts expire all existing sessions and codes. Terminate HTTPS at your server or reverse proxy. Configure the SMTP provider and a valid Origin Trial token before expecting live browser-issued tokens. Successful verification records the verified email in the server-side session; no separate account database existed in this starter app.
